[2026-03-14T23:49:56.097Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SBiM1QGxgAjh7hHhRoU2Dr","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a command-line tool for recording application sessions, gathering performance data, and debugging issues.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\nSet up authentication by creating an API key at https://app.replay.io settings and storing it in the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Essential Commands\n\nThe main commands include:\n\n- **`record [url]`** – Launches Replay Browser to record a session (defaults to `about:blank`)\n- **`upload [ids...]`** – Uploads recordings; without arguments opens an interactive menu\n- **`list`** – Shows all local recordings with ID, host, date, duration, and status\n- **`remove [ids...]`** – Deletes recordings (use `-a`/`--all` for all)\n- **`login`/`logout`** – Manages account authentication\n- **`upload-source-maps <paths...>`** – Uploads source maps with options for grouping and filtering\n\n## Typical Workflow\n\nRecord a session at a specific URL, then upload it:\n```sh\nreplayio record https://myapp.com\nreplayio upload <recording-id>\n```\n\nFor more details on any command, use the `-h` or `--help` flag."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1299eb81-ffa0-4f51-a2fd-69fb26f0839e","uuid":"8d2625f7-d520-4a6f-9f6b-5fbe5f9896fd","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a command-line tool for recording application sessions, gathering performance data, and debugging issues.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\nSet up authentication by creating an API key at https://app.replay.io settings and storing it in the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Essential Commands\n\nThe main commands include:\n\n- **`record [url]`** – Launches Replay Browser to record a session (defaults to `about:blank`)\n- **`upload [ids...]`** – Uploads recordings; without arguments opens an interactive menu\n- **`list`** – Shows all local recordings with ID, host, date, duration, and status\n- **`remove [ids...]`** – Deletes recordings (use `-a`/`--all` for all)\n- **`login`/`logout`** – Manages account authentication\n- **`upload-source-maps <paths...>`** – Uploads source maps with options for grouping and filtering\n\n## Typical Workflow\n\nRecord a session at a specific URL, then upload it:\n```sh\nreplayio record https://myapp.com\nreplayio upload <recording-id>\n```\n\nFor more details on any command, use the `-h` or `--help` flag.","durationMs":3733,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-14T23:49:56.962Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NLrFnoGkx26boKo6CJUPyS","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Overview\n\nReplay MCP is a tool designed to inspect contents of https://replay.io recordings, which capture browser activity with complete precision. According to the documentation, \"Any question about the app behaved can be answered precisely using data from the recording.\"\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nThe tool organizes information around two central ideas:\n\n**Points**: Specific moments in app execution where JS statements run or events with potential side effects occur.\n\n**Elements**: References to particular DOM elements independent of any specific point in time.\n\n## Tool Categories\n\nThe documentation divides available tools into two main groups:\n\n**Exploratory tools** discover what happened and when, including error tracking, timeline analysis, and source code examination.\n\n**Explanatory tools** clarify why events occurred, using dependency tracking and state inspection to show relationships between events.\n\n## Investigation Approach\n\nThe guidance recommends a methodical process: frame a specific question, explore related events, explain their causes, form a hypothesis supported by evidence, and communicate conclusions with data backing them up.\n\nThe Logpoint tool is highlighted as particularly valuable for tracking expression values across multiple statement executions to identify unexpected patterns requiring deeper investigation."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1299eb81-ffa0-4f51-a2fd-69fb26f0839e","uuid":"1d3e3c76-88a1-4c6f-8ea0-95fc4f320839","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Overview\n\nReplay MCP is a tool designed to inspect contents of https://replay.io recordings, which capture browser activity with complete precision. According to the documentation, \"Any question about the app behaved can be answered precisely using data from the recording.\"\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nThe tool organizes information around two central ideas:\n\n**Points**: Specific moments in app execution where JS statements run or events with potential side effects occur.\n\n**Elements**: References to particular DOM elements independent of any specific point in time.\n\n## Tool Categories\n\nThe documentation divides available tools into two main groups:\n\n**Exploratory tools** discover what happened and when, including error tracking, timeline analysis, and source code examination.\n\n**Explanatory tools** clarify why events occurred, using dependency tracking and state inspection to show relationships between events.\n\n## Investigation Approach\n\nThe guidance recommends a methodical process: frame a specific question, explore related events, explain their causes, form a hypothesis supported by evidence, and communicate conclusions with data backing them up.\n\nThe Logpoint tool is highlighted as particularly valuable for tracking expression values across multiple statement executions to identify unexpected patterns requiring deeper investigation.","durationMs":3700,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
